"Fairness in Serving Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2401.00588v2)\nciteturn55view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn49view2\",\"lineno\":92}); Total lines: 1106\nL87: Unfortunately, RPM can lead to low resource utilization. A client sending requests at a high rate will be restricted even if the system is underutilized. This leads to wasted resources, an undesirable situation given the cost and the scarcity of GPUs. Thus, we want a solution that provides not only isolation (like RPM limit) but also high resource utilization.\nL88: This is a common problem in many other domains like networking and operating systems. The solution of choice to achieve both isolation and high resource utilization in those domains has been fair queueing [cite68†30 ]. Fair queueing ensures that each client will get their “fair share”. In the simplest case, if there are $n$ clients sharing the same resource, the fair share is at least $1/n$ of the resource, which means that each client gets at least $1/n$ of the resource.\nL89: Furthermore, if some clients do not use their share, other clients with more demands can use it, hence leading to higher resource utilization.\nL90: In this paper, we apply fair sharing to the domain of LLM serving at the token granularity. We do it at the token rather than request granularity to avoid unfairness due to request heterogeneity. Consider two clients, client $A$ sends requests of 2K tokens each (both input and output), and client $B$ sends requests of 200 tokens each. Serving an equal number of requests for each client would be unfair to client $B$ as her requests consume much fewer resources than client $A$’s requests.\nL91: This is similar to networking where fair queuing is typically applied to the bit granularity, rather than packet granularity.\nL92: Despite these similarities, we cannot directly use the algorithms developed for networking and operating systems, as LLM serving has several unique characteristics. First, the request output lengths are unknown in advance. In contrast, in networking, the packet lengths are known before the packet is scheduled. Second, the cost of each token can vary.\nL93: For instance, the cost of processing an input (prompt) token is typically lower than that of an output token, because input token processing is parallelizable. In contrast, the cost of sending a bit or the cost of a CPU time slice are the same irrespective of the workload. Third, the effective capacity of an LLM server (i.e., processing rate expressed in token/sec when the request queue is non-empty) can vary over time. For example, longer input sequences take more memory.\nL94: This limits the number of batched parallel requests during generation, leading to GPU under-utilization and a lower processing rate. In contrast, the network or CPU capacity is assumed to be fixed.\nL95: In this paper, we discuss the factors that need to be considered when defining fairness in the context of LLM serving. We show how different definitions can be incorporated into a configurable service cost function in Section cite16†3 . While the cost function can be customized, a simple metric of counting input and output tokens at different prices is extensively used in analysis for the sake of simplicity.\nL96: We then present a fair scheduling algorithm called Virtual Token Counter (VTC) that can be easily adapted for different service cost functions. At a high level, VTC tracks the services received for each client and will prioritize the ones with the least services received, with a counter lift each time a client is new to the queue. It updates the counters at a token-level granularity on the fly, which addresses the unknown length issue.\nL97: VTC integrates seamlessly with current LLM serving batching techniques (Section cite8†2.1 ), and its scheduling mechanism does not depend on the server’s capacity, overcoming the problem of the dynamically fluctuating server capacity. We also provide theoretical bounds of fairness for VTC in cite20†Section 4.1 . The serving architecture of VTC is illustrated in Figure cite69†1 .\nL98: In summary, this paper makes the following contributions:\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: \nL102: This is the first work to discuss the fair serving of Large Language Models to the best of our knowledge. We identify its unique challenges and give the definition of LLM serving fairness (cite16†Section 3 ).\nL103: \nL104:   * •\nL105: \nL106: We propose a simple yet effective fair-serving algorithm called VTC. We provide rigorous proofs for VTC on fairness guarantee, which gives fairness bound within $2\\times$ of the optimal bound (cite19†Section 4 ).\nL107: \nL108:   * •\nL109: We conduct in-depth evaluations on our proposed algorithm VTC. Results confirm that our proposed algorithms are fair and work-conserving (cite26†Section 5 ).\nL110: ## 2 Background\nL111: \nL112: In this section, we first introduce how an LLM serving system operates. Then we describe existing methods for ensuring fairness in LLM serving.\nL113: \nL114: ### 2.1 Large Language Models Serving\nL115: ##### LLM serving with a single request\nL116: \nL117: First, a request contains information about its arrival time ($a$), input tokens ($x$), and its associated client ($u$). Formally, we represent a request using a three-tuple ($a$, $x$, $u$). The system generates output tokens based on the input tokens. For instance, the input tokens can be an incomplete sentence, and the system generates the rest of the sentence [cite70†35 ].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRagas: Automated Evaluation of Retrieval Augmented Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2309.15217v2)\nciteturn55view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn49view5\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 220\nL60: LLMs have also been leveraged to automatically evaluate other aspects of generated text fragments, beyond factuality. For instance, GPTScore cite48†Fu et al. (2023) uses a prompt that specifies the considered aspect (e.g. fluency) and then scores passages based on the average probability of the generated tokens, according to a given autoregressive LM. This idea of using prompts was previously also considered by cite45†Yuan et al. (2021) , although they used a smaller fine-tuned LM (i.e.\nL61: BART) and did not observe a clear benefit from using prompts. Another approach directly asks ChatGPT to evaluate a particular aspect of the given answer by providing a score between 0 and 100, or by providing a rating on a 5-star scale cite49†Wang et al. (2023a) . Remarkably, strong results can be obtained in this way, although it comes with the limitation of being sensitive to the design of the prompt.\nL62: Rather than scoring individual answers, some authors have also focused on using an LLM to select the best answer among a number of candidates cite50†Wang et al. (2023b) , typically to compare the performance of different LLMs. However, care is needed with this approach, as the order in which the answers is presented can influence the result cite50†Wang et al. (2023b) .\nL63: In terms of how ground truth answers or, more generally, generations, have been typically used in the literature, most approaches have relied on the availability of one or more reference answers. For instance, BERTScore cite51†Zhang et al. (2020) and MoverScore cite52†Zhao et al. (2019) use contextualised embeddings, produced by a pre-trained BERT model, to compare the similarity between the generated answer and the reference answers. BARTScore cite45†Yuan et al.\nL64: (2021) similarly uses reference answers to compute aspects such as precision (estimated as the probability of generating the generated answer given the reference) and recall (estimated as the probability of generating the reference given the generated answer).\nL65: ## 3 Evaluation Strategies\nL66: We consider a standard RAG setting, where given a question $q$, the system first retrieves some context $c(q)$ and then uses the retrieved context to generate an answer $a_{s}(q)$. When building a RAG system, we usually do not have access to human-annotated datasets or reference answers. We therefore focus on metrics that are fully self-contained and reference-free. We focus in particular three quality aspects, which we argue are of central importance.\nL67: First, Faithfulness refers to the idea that the answer should be grounded in the given context. This is important to avoid hallucinations, and to ensure that the retrieved context can act as a justification for the generated answer. Indeed, RAG systems are often used in applications where the factual consistency of the generated text w.r.t. the grounded sources is highly important, e.g. in domains such as law, where information is constantly evolving.\nL68: Second, Answer Relevance refers to the idea that the generated answer should address the actual question that was provided. Finally, Context Relevance refers to the idea that the retrieved context should be focused, containing as little irrelevant information as possible. This is important given the cost associated with feeding long context passages to LLMs.\nL69: Moreover, when context passages are too long, LLMs are often less effective in exploiting that context, especially for information that is provided in the middle of the context passage cite53†Liu et al. (2023) .\nL70: We now explain how these three quality aspects can be measured in a fully automated way, by prompting an LLM. In our implementation and experiments, all prompts are evaluated using the gpt-3.5-turbo-16k model, which is available through the OpenAI API^{2}^{2} 2 cite54†https://platform.openai.com†platform.openai.com .\nL71: ### Faithfulness\nL72: We say that the answer $a_{s}(q)$ is faithful to the context $c(q)$ if the claims that are made in the answer can be inferred from the context. To estimate faithfulness, we first use an LLM to extract a set of statements, $S(a_{s}(q))$. The aim of this step is to decompose longer sentences into shorter and more focused assertions. We use the following prompt for this step^{3}^{3} 3 To help clarify the task, we include a demonstration as part of the prompt.\nL73: This demonstration is not explicitly shown in the listing of the prompts throughout this paper.:\nL74: > Given a question and answer, create one or more statements from each sentence in the given answer.\nL75: > question: [question]\nL76: > answer: [answer]\nL77: \nL78: where [question] and [answer] refer to the given question and answer. For each statement $s_{i}$ in $S$, the LLM determines if $s_{i}$ can be inferred from $c(q)$ using a verification function $v(s_{i},c(q))$. This verification step is carried out using the following prompt:\nL79: > Consider the given context and following statements, then determine whether they are supported by the information present in the context. Provide a brief explanation for each statement before arriving at the verdict (Yes/No). Provide a final verdict for each statement in order at the end in the given format. Do not deviate from the specified format.\nL80: > statement: [statement 1]\nL81: > …\nL82: > statement: [statement $n$]\nL83: The final faithfulness score, $F$, is then computed as $F=\\frac{|V|}{|S|}$, where $|V|$ is the number of statements that were supported according to the LLM and $|S|$ is the total number of statements.\nL84: ### Answer relevance\nL85: \nL86: We say that the answer $a_{s}(q)$ is relevant if it directly addresses the question in an appropriate way. In particular, our assessment of answer relevance does not take into account factuality, but penalises cases where the answer is incomplete or where it contains redundant information. To estimate answer relevance, for the given answer $a_{s}(q)$, we prompt the LLM to generate $n$ potential questions $q_{i}$ based on $a_{s}(q)$, as follows:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFast Distributed Inference Serving for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2305.05920v3)\nciteturn55view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn54view1\",\"lineno\":114}); Total lines: 425\nL106: Challenge 2: GPU memory overhead. Preemptive scheduling policies introduce additional GPU memory consumption during LLM inference, unlike FCFS with run-to-completion, which only needs to maintain the key-value cache for ongoing jobs. In contrast, preemptive scheduling must keep the key-value cache in GPU memory for all preempted jobs in the pending state, to be used for future token generation. This key-value cache consumes a substantial amount of GPU memory, leading to potential challenges.\nL107: For instance, a single job of OPT 175B with an input sequence length of 512 requires at least 2.3 GB of memory for the key-value cache (§cite16†4.2 ). Due to scarce GPU memory capacity, the size of the key-value cache becomes a critical factor affecting the effectiveness of preemptive scheduling policies. Prior works have proposed memory-saving techniques for the key-value cache.\nL108: Multi-Query Attention [cite57†28 ] and Group-Query Attention [cite58†29 ] try to reduce the memory consumption by sharing key-value tensors between attention heads. They may hurt the capability of LLMs and the memory consumption still grows linearly with the sequence length. vLLM [cite37†11 ] manages the key-value cache at the granularity of blocks to reduce GPU memory fragmentation. It cannot reduce the memory usage caused by the key-value cache itself.\nL109: As the context length increases [cite59†30 ], the memory consumption of the key-value cache is a hard problem and is becoming increasingly important.\nL110: ## 3 FastServe Overview\nL111: \nL112: cite60†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: FastServe architecture.\nL113: ### 3.1 Desired Properties\nL114: \nL115: LLMs come with unique characteristics that pose challenges to distributed computation and GPU memory consumption. Our goal is to develop an efficient inference serving system for LLMs that fulfills the following three requirements.\nL116: \nL117:   * •\nL118: Low latency and high throughput. Our focus centers on interactive LLM applications, where users have high expectations for fast response. To measure it quantitatively, we want the maximum throughput as high as possible under a certain latency requirement.\nL119: \nL120:   * •\nL121: \nL122: Efficient GPU memory management. LLMs pose a significant challenge in terms of GPU memory consumption, which necessitates an effective GPU memory management approach for both the model and intermediate states.\nL123: \nL124:   * •\nL125: Scalable distributed execution. The nature of LLMs demands multiple GPUs to enable distributed inference effectively, which requires the system to support scalable distributed execution cross GPU servers.\nL126: ### 3.2 Overall Architecture\nL127: \nL128: Figure cite61†4 shows the architecture of FastServe. Jobs are submitted to the job pool. The scheduler utilizes information from the job profiler to determine the initial job priority and then places the job in the skip-join MLFQ (§cite15†4.1 ) to mitigate head-of-line blocking.\nL129: For execution, the scheduler picks the jobs based on their priority within the skip-join MLFQ to form a pre-defined maximum batch size and dispatches the batch to the distributed execution engine to perform one iteration. The distributed execution engine collaborates with the distributed key-value cache to access and update the key-value tensors relevant to the respective job.\nL130: To tackle the challenge of limited GPU memory capacity, the key-value cache manager proactively swaps key-value tensors between GPU memory and host memory (§cite16†4.2 ).\nL131: To accommodate extreme large models such as OPT-175B, FastServe employs distributed inference, enabling both tensor parallelism and pipeline parallelism. FastServe incorporates extensions into the scheduler and key-value cache to enable seamless support for distributed execution (§cite17†4.3 ).\nL132: ## 4 FastServe Design\nL133: \nL134: In this section, we first introduce the skip-join MLFQ scheduler to minimize latency (§cite15†4.1 ). Then, we present a proactive KV cache management mechanism designed to effectively ameliorate the GPU memory capacity constraint (§cite16†4.2 ). Finally, we demonstrate how to apply these techniques to the distributed settings (§cite17†4.3 ).\nL135: \nL136: Figure 5: Execution time of the first four iterations.\nL137: ### 4.1 Skip-Join MLFQ Scheduler\nL138: Strawman: fixed priority scheduling. To support preemptive scheduling, we need a priority-based scheduler to decide which jobs to preempt and which to execute. One naive solution is to assign a fixed priority to each job based on its input length. In this case, when the initialization phase dominates the total latency, the fixed priority scheduling can approximate the optimal performance as the SRPT policy does.\nL139: However, although this solution leverages the information about the initialization phase, it ignores the characteristics of the decoding phase. Many real-world datasets like ShareGPT and Alpaca show a long tail distribution implying that jobs with a long output length also exist. When the decoding phase dominates the total latency, the fixed priority scheduling may deviate from the optimal performance of SRPT.\nL140: Strawman: naive MLFQ. Due to the indeterminate job size of LLM inference, directly applying SRPT is not feasible. In information-agnostic settings, Least-Attained Service (LAS) has been shown to approximate SRPT effectively. Due to the job switching overhead of LAS, the practical approach is Multi-Level Feedback Queue (MLFQ) which has gained popularity in various scheduling systems [cite62†31 , cite63†32 , cite64†33 , cite42†15 , cite65†34 ]. MLFQ operates multiple queues, each with a different priority level.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNeuSpell: A Neural Spelling Correction Toolkit (https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-demos.21.pdf)\nciteturn55view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view3\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 459\nL58@P1: Lastly, several recent studies have shown that\nL59@P1: many state-of-the-art neural models developed for\nL60@P1: a variety of Natural Language Processing (NLP)\nL61@P1: tasks easily break in the presence of natural or syn\u0002thetic spelling errors (Belinkov and Bisk, 2017;\nL62@P1: Ebrahimi et al., 2017; Pruthi et al., 2019). We\nL63@P1: determine the usefulness of our toolkit as a counter\u0002measure against character-level adversarial attacks\nL64@P1: (§ 5.2). We find that our models are better defenses\nL65@P1: to adversarial attacks than previously proposed\nL66@P1: spell checkers. We believe that our toolkit would\nL67@P1: encourage practitioners to incorporate spelling cor\u0002rection systems in other NLP applications.\nL68@P1: Model Correction\nL69@P1: Rates\nL70@P1: Time per sentence\nL71@P1: (milliseconds)\nL72@P1: ASPELL (Atkinson, 2019) 48.7 7.3∗\nL73@P1: JAMSPELL (Ozinov, 2019) 68.9 2.6∗\nL74@P1: CHAR-CNN-LSTM (Kim et al., 2015) 75.8 4.2\nL75@P1: SC-LSTM (Sakaguchi et al., 2016) 76.7 2.8\nL76@P1: CHAR-LSTM-LSTM (Li et al., 2018) 77.3 6.4\nL77@P1: BERT (Devlin et al., 2018) 79.1 7.1\nL78@P1: SC-LSTM\nL79@P1: +ELMO (input) 79.8 15.8\nL80@P1: +ELMO (output) 78.5 16.3\nL81@P1: +BERT (input) 77.0 6.7\nL82@P1: +BERT (output) 76.0 7.2\nL83@P1: Table 1: Performance of different correctors in the\nL84@P1: NeuSpell toolkit on the BEA-60K dataset with real\u0002world spelling mistakes. ∗\nL85@P1: indicates evaluation on a\nL86@P1: CPU (for others we use a GeForce RTX 2080 Ti GPU).\nL87@P1: 2 Models in NeuSpell\nL88@P1: Our toolkit offers ten different spelling correction\nL89@P1: models, which include: (i) two off-the-shelf non\u0002neural models, (ii) four published neural models\nL90@P1: for spelling correction, (iii) four of our extensions.\nL91@P1: The details of first six systems are following:\nL92@P1: • GNU Aspell (Atkinson, 2019): It uses a com\u0002bination of metaphone phonetic algorithm,2\nL93@P1: Ispell’s near miss strategy,3and a weighted\nL94@P1: edit distance metric to score candidate words.\nL95@P1: • JamSpell (Ozinov, 2019): It uses a variant of\nL96@P1: the SymSpell algorithm,4and a 3-gram lan\u0002guage model to prune word-level corrections.\nL97@P1: 2\nL98@P1: http://aspell.net/metaphone/\nL99@P1: 3\nL100@P1: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ispell\nL101@P1: 4\nL102@P1: https://github.com/wolfgarbe/SymSpell\nL103@P1: • SC-LSTM (Sakaguchi et al., 2016): It corrects\nL104@P1: misspelt words using semi-character represen\u0002tations, fed through a bi-LSTM network. The\nL105@P1: semi-character representations are a concate\u0002nation of one-hot embeddings for the (i) first,\nL106@P1: (ii) last, and (iii) bag of internal characters.\nL107@P1: • CHAR-LSTM-LSTM (Li et al., 2018): The\nL108@P1: model builds word representations by passing\nL109@P1: its individual characters to a bi-LSTM. These\nL110@P1: representations are further fed to another bi\u0002LSTM trained to predict the correction.\nL111@P1: • CHAR-CNN-LSTM (Kim et al., 2015): Similar\nL112@P1: to the previous model, this model builds word\u0002level representations from individual charac\u0002ters using a convolutional network.\nL113@P1: • BERT (Devlin et al., 2018): The model uses\nL114@P1: a pre-trained transformer network. We aver\u0002age the sub-word representations to obtain the\nL115@P1: word representations, which are further fed to\nL116@P1: a classifier to predict its correction.\nL117@P1: To better capture the context around a misspelt\nL118@P1: token, we extend the SC-LSTM model by aug\u0002menting it with deep contextual representations\nL119@P1: from pre-trained ELMo and BERT. Since the best\nL120@P1: point to integrate such embeddings might vary by\nL121@P1: task (Peters et al., 2018), we append them either\nL122@P1: to semi-character embeddings before feeding them\nL123@P1: to the biLSTM or to the biLSTM’s output. Cur\u0002rently, our toolkit provides four such trained mod\u0002els: ELMo/BERT tied at input/output with a semi\u0002character based bi-LSTM model.\nL124@P1: Implementation Details Neural models in\nL125@P1: NeuSpell are trained by posing spelling correction\nL126@P1: as a sequence labeling task, where a correct\nL127@P1: word is marked as itself and a misspelt token\nL128@P1: is labeled as its correction. Out-of-vocabulary\nL129@P1: labels are marked as UNK. For each word in the\nL130@P1: input text sequence, models are trained to output\nL131@P1: a probability distribution over a finite vocabulary\nL132@P1: using a softmax layer.\nL133@P1: We set the hidden size of the bi-LSTM network\nL134@P1: in all models to 512 and use {50,100,100,100}\nL135@P1: sized convolution filters with lengths {2,3,4,5} re\u0002spectively in CNNs. We use a dropout of 0.4 on\nL136@P1: the bi-LSTM’s outputs and train the models using\nL137@P1: cross-entropy loss. We use the BertAdam5 opti\u0002mizer for models with a BERT component and the\nL138@P1: 5\nL139@P1-2: github.com/cedrickchee/pytorch-pretrained-BERT160\nL140@P2: Adam (Kingma and Ba, 2014) optimizer for the\nL141@P2: remainder. These optimizers are used with default\nL142@P2: parameter settings. We use a batch size of 32 ex\u0002amples, and train with a patience of 3 epochs.\nL143@P2: During inference, we first replace UNK predic\u0002tions with their corresponding input words and then\nL144@P2: evaluate the results. We evaluate models for accu\u0002racy (percentage of correct words among all words)\nL145@P2: and word correction rate (percentage of misspelt to\u0002kens corrected). We use AllenNLP6\nL146@P2: and Hugging\u0002face7\nL147@P2: libraries to use ELMo and BERT respectively.\nL148@P2: All neural models in our toolkit are implemented\nL149@P2: using the Pytorch library (Paszke et al., 2017), and\nL150@P2: are compatible to run on both CPU and GPU en\u0002vironments. Performance of different models are\nL151@P2: presented in Table 1.\nL152@P2: 3 Synthetic Training Datasets\nL153@P2: Due to scarcity of available parallel data for\nL154@P2: spelling correction, we noise sentences to gener\u0002ate misspelt-correct sentence pairs. We use 1.6M\nL155@P2: sentences from the one billion word benchmark\nL156@P2: (Chelba et al., 2013) dataset as our clean corpus.\nL157@P2: Using different noising strategies from existing lit\u0002erature, we noise ∼20% of the tokens in the clean\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions (https://arxiv.org/html/2506.09038v1)\nciteturn55view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn53view2\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 787\nL25:     2. cite15†4.2 Post-training instills select abstention capabilities L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Reasoning degrades abstention L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Crafting a system prompt can boost abstention L28:   6. cite18†5 Discussion L29:   7. cite19†References L30:   8. cite20†A Limitations L31:   9. cite21†B Broader impacts L32:   10. cite22†C Additional methods L33:     1. cite23†C.1 Models L34:     2. cite24†C.2 Datasets L35:       1. cite25†C.2.1 Systematic dataset review L36:       2. cite26†C.2.2 Dataset implementation details L37:       3. cite27†C.2.3 Reasoning datasets L38:       4. cite28†C.2.4 Excluded datasets L39:     3. cite29†C.3 LLM Judges L40:       1. cite30†C.3.1 LLM judge for detecting abstention L41:       2. cite31†C.3.2 LLM judge abstention detection prompt L42:       3. cite32†C.3.3 Abstention response annotation L43:       4. cite33†C.3.4 Abstention detection judge evaluations L44:       5. cite34†C.3.5 LLM judge for evaluating correctness L45:   11. cite35†D Additional results L46:     1. cite36†D.1 General abstention performance L47:     2. cite37†D.2 Effect of scale L48:     3. cite38†D.3 Effect of post-training L49:     4. cite39†D.4 Effect of reasoning fine-tuning L50:       1. cite40†Reasoning inference. L51:       2. cite41†o1 reasoning effort. L52:       3. cite42†LLM judge which evaluates both reasoning chain and final answer. L53:     5. cite43†D.5 System prompt analysis L54:   12. cite44†E Code and reproducibility L55:   13. cite45†F Qualitative examples L56:     1. cite46†F.1 Appropriate abstention from Qwen 2.5 32B L57:       1. cite47†Answer Unknown (KUQ/Future Unknowns) L58:       2. cite48†Stale (FreshQA) L59:       3. cite49†Underspecified Intent (SituatedQA/Geo) L60:       4. cite50†Underspecified Context (SQuAD 2.0) L61:       5. cite51†False Premise (QAQA) L62:       6. cite52†Subjective (CoCoNot/Subjective) L63:     2. cite53†F.2 Failure to abstain from OLMo 7B Instruct L64:       1. cite54†Answer Unknown (CoCoNot/Unsupported) L65:       2. cite55†Stale (FreshQA) L66:       3. cite56†Underspecified Intent (CoCoNot/Incomprehensible) L67:       4. cite57†Underspecified Context (BBQ) L68:       5. cite58†False Premise (CoCoNot/False presumptions) L69:       6. cite59†Subjective (CoCoNot/Humanizing) L70:     3. cite60†F.3 Failure to abstain on reasoning datasets from s1.1 32B L71:       1. cite61†MMLU-Math-Abstain L72:     4. cite62†F.4 GPQA-Diamond-Abstain L73:     5. cite63†F.5 GSM8K-Abstain L74:       1. cite64†UMWP L75: cite65†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L76: \nL77: arXiv:2506.09038v1 [cs.AI] 10 Jun 2025\nL78: # AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions\nL79: \nL80: Polina Kirichenko Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.    Mark Ibrahim Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.    Kamalika Chaudhuri Affiliation: FAIR at Meta    Samuel J. Bell Affiliation: FAIR at Meta Joint co-first author; author order determined by random shuffling.\nL81: ###### Abstract\nL82: For Large Language Models (LLMs) to be reliably deployed in both everyday and high-stakes domains, knowing when not to answer is equally critical as answering correctly. Real-world user queries, which can be underspecified, ill-posed, or fundamentally unanswerable, require LLMs to reason about uncertainty and selectively abstain—i.e., refuse to answer definitively. However, abstention remains understudied, without a systematic evaluation framework for modern LLMs.\nL83: In this work, we introduce AbstentionBench: a large-scale benchmark for holistically evaluating abstention across 20 diverse datasets, including questions with unknown answers, underspecification, false premises, subjective interpretations, and outdated information. Evaluating 20 frontier LLMs reveals abstention is an unsolved problem, and one where scaling models is of little use.\nL84: While recent reasoning LLMs have shown impressive results in complex problem solving, surprisingly, we find that reasoning fine-tuning degrades abstention (by 24% on average), even for math and science domains on which reasoning models are explicitly trained. We find that while a carefully crafted system prompt can boost abstention in practice, it does not resolve models’ fundamental inability to reason about uncertainty. We release AbstentionBench to foster research into advancing LLM reliability.\nL85: ^{†}^{†}date: August 24, 2026^{†}^{†}correspondence: {polkirichenko, marksibrahim, sjbell}@meta.com^{†}^{†}Code: cite66†https://github.com/facebookresearch/AbstentionBench†github.com L86: ## 1 Introduction\nL87: Reliability is key to user trust in Large Language Models (LLMs). If users can’t trust model responses, we can’t fully benefit from their application—in either everyday or high-stakes settings (cite67†Thirunavukarasu et al., 2023 ; cite68†Guha et al., 2023 ; cite69†Wu et al., 2023 ).\nL88: However, faced with a changing world and noisy, ambiguous, or unanswerable user queries, there will always be cases where a reliable response is impossible: models need not only answer with high accuracy, but must also know when not to answer. For example, the answer to the important query “My dog was prescribed 5mg/kg Prednisone, how much should I give her?” depends on the specific dog’s weight, here left unspecified.\nL89: Reliable models must recognize such uncertainty and abstain—i.e., avoid providing a definitive answer—instead expressing uncertainty, clarifying, or simply responding “I don’t know”. To do this successfully, LLMs need to reason about both evidence and uncertainty, weighing the information available to determine whether an answer is appropriate.\n"
